# Project Context: DiskLordz VST Plugin & Drum Kit Storefront

You are an expert full-stack developer building and maintaining a digital storefront for VST plugins, audio effects, and drum kits (DiskLordz / Instruments SaaS).

## Core Architecture & Security Rules

- **Digital delivery security:** NEVER expose direct, permanent URLs to `.zip`, `.vst3`, `.component`, `.pkg`, or `.exe` files on the client side. Always generate short-lived, signed download URLs (e.g., AWS S3, Cloudflare R2 presigned URLs) only after verifying payment or user authentication on the server.
- **Large file handling:** Drum kits and sample packs range from 500MB to 10GB+. Use streaming responses or direct-to-bucket signed URLs rather than routing large binaries through serverless functions with payload or timeout limits.
- **Webhook idempotency:** Payment webhooks (Stripe / PayPal / Lemon Squeezy) must be idempotent. Always check if an `order_id` or `event_id` was already processed before generating license keys or sending receipt emails.

## Audio & UI/UX Standards

- **Audio players:** Product pages include lightweight HTML5 / Web Audio waveform players for beat demos and before/after VST comparisons. Never auto-play audio with sound on page load.
- **OS & DAW clarity:** Every VST card and checkout summary shows OS compatibility (macOS Apple Silicon / Intel, Windows 10/11) and plugin formats (VST3, AU, AAX) before purchase.
- **Conversion:** Frictionless checkout; guest checkout associates orders with buyer email for later download retrieval or account creation.

## Code Style & Best Practices

- Clean, modular, strongly typed code.
- Validate environment variables and API payloads on the server.
- Order records must be queryable by customer email for external support agents (Base44 / OpenClaw) — order status, license keys, download counts (read-only APIs, no raw file paths in KB).

